{"date": "2026-10-28", "post_id": 1503917, "title": "The Mutual Occultation of Ganymede and Io", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-28-the-mutual-occultation-of-ganymede-and-io/", "media_type": "video", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> Jupiter is one of the least lonely planets in the Solar System, at least when it comes to <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/moons/\">natural satellites</a>. <a href=\"https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/how-many-moons/en/\">115 moons</a> have been discovered orbiting Jupiter so far! (<em>Saturn has the most with 293, but <a href=\"https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/sats/discovery.html\">who\u2019s counting</a>.</em>) <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/jupiter-moons/ganymede/\">Ganymede</a> and <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/jupiter-moons/io/\">Io</a>, two of Jupiter\u2019s <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/general/415-years-ago-astronomer-galileo-discovers-jupiters-moons/\">Galilean moons</a>, met in the sky on October 20th, 2026. Today\u2019s video captures the meeting, with <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2021-june-14-ganymede-from-juno/\">Ganymede</a> briefly blocking <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-january-20-io-in-true-color/\">Io</a> from view. This event, when an astronomical object passes in front of another and either completely or partially blocks it, is called a mutual occultation. The <a href=\"https://www.alpo-astronomy.org/jupiter\">mutual occultations of Galilean moons</a> are rare to witness because they require Earth\u2019s edge-on view of their orbits. This occurs once every 6 years when Jupiter reaches <a href=\"https://earthsky.org/astronomy-essentials/jupiter-equinox-mutual-events-moon-2021/\">its equinox</a> and appears less \u201ctilted\u201d. Ganymede is also <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2022-april-27-moon-shadow-on-jupiter/\">casting its shadow</a> on Jupiter\u2019s right side. Any <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jupiter_in_fiction\">fictional Jovian species</a> within the shadow would experience a <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-august-17-a-golden-corona-eclipse/\">solar eclipse</a>!<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> a planet grows horns", "credit": "Michael Wong", "copyright": "<a href=\"https://www.astromw.com/\">Michael Wong</a>", "alt": "The disk of Jupiter sits to the right. The giant red spot rotates into view and the horizontal cloud streams swirl. To the left, Ganymede passes in front of Io. An inlet shows this zoomed in. Ganymede casts a shadow on Jupiter&#039;s disk.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-28-the-mutual-occultation-of-ganymede-and-io/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/occultation.jpeg?w=1600&h=1000&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 October 28 - The Mutual Occultation of Ganymede and Io</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-28-the-mutual-occultation-of-ganymede-and-io/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: Jupiter is one of the least lonely planets in the Solar System, at least when it comes to natural satellites. 115 moons have been discovered orbiting Jupiter so far! (Saturn has the most with 293, but who\u2019s counting.) Ganymede and Io, two of Jupiter\u2019s Galilean moons, met in the sky on October 20th, 2026. Today\u2019s video captures the meeting, with Ganymede briefly blocking Io from view. This event, when an astronomical object passes in front of another and either completely or partially blocks it, is called a mutual occultation. The mutual occultations of Galilean moons are rare to witness because they require Earth\u2019s edge-on view of their orbits. This occurs once every 6 years when Jupiter reaches its equinox and appears less \u201ctilted\u201d. Ganymede is also casting its shadow on Jupiter\u2019s right side. Any fictional Jovian species within the shadow would experience a solar eclipse!Tomorrow&#039;s picture: a planet grows horns\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"the mutual occultation of ganymede and io\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"The Mutual Occultation of Ganymede and Io\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-28-the-mutual-occultation-of-ganymede-and-io/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/occultation.jpeg?w=1600&#038;h=1000&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/occultation.jpeg?w=1600&#038;h=1000&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"The disk of Jupiter sits to the right. Large horizontal bands of clouds are visible on Jupiter and the giant red spot is rotating into view. To the left, Ganymede and Io orbit Jupiter. An inlet shows this zoomed in. Ganymede casts a shadow on Jupiter&#039;s disk.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-10-28T00:05:00-04:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-10-27T12:22:11-04:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"The Mutual Occultation of Ganymede and Io\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/occultation.jpeg?w=1600&#038;h=1000&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 October 28<br>\n\n<video controls title=\"\">\n<source src=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/occultation.mp4\" type=\"video/mp4\">\n</video>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> The Mutual Occultation of Ganymede and Io </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\nMichael Wong\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> Jupiter is one of the least lonely planets in the Solar System, at least when it comes to <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/moons/\">natural satellites</a>. <a href=\"https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/how-many-moons/en/\">115 moons</a> have been discovered orbiting Jupiter so far! (<em>Saturn has the most with 293, but <a href=\"https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/sats/discovery.html\">who\u2019s counting</a>.</em>) <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/jupiter-moons/ganymede/\">Ganymede</a> and <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/jupiter-moons/io/\">Io</a>, two of Jupiter\u2019s <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/general/415-years-ago-astronomer-galileo-discovers-jupiters-moons/\">Galilean moons</a>, met in the sky on October 20th, 2026. Today\u2019s video captures the meeting, with <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2021-june-14-ganymede-from-juno/\">Ganymede</a> briefly blocking <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-january-20-io-in-true-color/\">Io</a> from view. This event, when an astronomical object passes in front of another and either completely or partially blocks it, is called a mutual occultation. The <a href=\"https://www.alpo-astronomy.org/jupiter\">mutual occultations of Galilean moons</a> are rare to witness because they require Earth\u2019s edge-on view of their orbits. This occurs once every 6 years when Jupiter reaches <a href=\"https://earthsky.org/astronomy-essentials/jupiter-equinox-mutual-events-moon-2021/\">its equinox</a> and appears less \u201ctilted\u201d. Ganymede is also <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2022-april-27-moon-shadow-on-jupiter/\">casting its shadow</a> on Jupiter\u2019s right side. Any <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jupiter_in_fiction\">fictional Jovian species</a> within the shadow would experience a <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-august-17-a-golden-corona-eclipse/\">solar eclipse</a>!<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> a planet grows horns\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-27-noctilucent-clouds-over-sweden/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nKeighley Rockcliffe, Robert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261028/html"}